  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Thursday: Upper-level ridging has begun \nstrengthening over the Inland Northwest, and will bring drier and \nwarmer conditions over the next couple days. Combined with the warm \nfront passage yesterday, temperatures will be much higher than \nnormal for this time of year. Today (Wednesday) and tomorrow will \nsee temperatures in the mid to high 50s, more than 10 degrees above \nnormal than what we usually see in mid-November. Though drier air is \nstill moving into the area, moisture left over from the rain \nyesterday resulted in dense fog this morning. Boundary layer \nmoisture is high enough to bring a returning chance of fog on \nThursday morning as well, which could impact morning commutes. \n\nFriday through Tuesday: A series of low pressure systems will \nmove through the area Friday through the beginning of next week \nas the ridge moves eastward. The first system will move through \nFriday into Saturday, and the second will move through Sunday \ninto Monday. Ensemble cluster agreement is strong for both \nsystems and throughout the extended period, with only minor \ndifferences in track and strength. Temperatures will moderate \nslightly as these systems usher in colder air, with highs in the\n50s rather than the low to mid 40s. The second system (Sunday\nthrough Tuesday) looks to bring more precipitation to the \nInland Northwest than the first. Snow levels will start out at \naround 7000 feet, but then by Saturday they will lower to 5000, \nand by Monday will drop to 4000 feet. This will bring snow to \nmountain passes, but totals have dropped since yesterday. \nThrough Tuesday morning, Washington Pass is forecast to see the\nhighest snowfall totals, with 3 to 6 inches possible, and \nStevens Pass looks to see around an inch. Rainfall amounts \nrange from 0.25-0.4 inches in the lowlands to up to 0.75 inches \nin the mountains. Winds in the Columbia Basin will increase \nFriday through Sunday as the systems move through, with wind \ngusts 20-25 mph. /AS",
